            <a href="#mock-strategy">6. Mock-Test Strategy</a>
            <a href="#preparation">7. Preparation Plan</a>
            <a href="#faq">8. FAQs</a>
          </aside>

          <article class="article">
            <p class="lead">
              The JPSC Preliminary Examination should be treated as more than
              a list of subjects. A strong preparation plan connects the
              official syllabus with previous year questions, revision and
              timed mock-test practice.
            </p>

            <h2 id="exam-pattern">JPSC Prelims Exam Pattern</h2>

            <p>
              The published JPSC scheme provides for two compulsory objective
              papers: General Studies Paper-I and General Studies Paper-II.
              Each paper carries 200 marks and has a duration of two hours.
              The published syllabus specifies 100 objective questions in
              each paper, with each question carrying two marks.
            </p>

            <div class="source-note">
              <strong>Official basis:</strong>
              The JPSC Combined Civil Services examination advertisement
              published by the Commission states that the Preliminary
              Examination consists of two compulsory papers of 200 marks each.
              Paper-I contains 100 objective questions of 2 marks each and a
              2-hour duration.
            </div>

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Paper</th>
                    <th>Subject</th>
                    <th>Questions</th>
                    <th>Marks</th>
                    <th>Time</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Paper-I</td>
                    <td>General Studies-I</td>
                    <td>100</td>
                    <td>200</td>
                    <td>2 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Paper-II</td>
                    <td>General Studies-II</td>
                    <td>100</td>
                    <td>200</td>
                    <td>2 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="total-row">
                    <td colspan="2">Total</td>
                    <td>200</td>
                    <td>400</td>
                    <td>4 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h3>Negative marking</h3>

            <p>
              The published JPSC examination scheme states that there is no
              negative marking for an incorrect answer in the Preliminary
              Examination. If a candidate marks more than one answer for a
              question, no marks are awarded for that question. Leaving a
              question blank carries no penalty.
            </p>

            <div class="strategy-box">
              <div class="strategy-label">HABITAT IAS TIP</div>
              <h3>No negative marking does not mean blind guessing.</h3>
              <p>
                Use elimination and probability intelligently. Your target
                should still be high accuracy because time, uncertainty and
                question quality matter even when there is no deduction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2 id="paper-one">JPSC Prelims Paper-I Syllabus</h2>

            <p>
              General Studies Paper-I is the broad national and general-studies
              component of the Preliminary Examination. The official 2024
              JPSC syllabus gives the following question distribution for the
              major listed areas.
            </p>

            <div class="weight-grid">
              <div>
                <strong>15</strong>
                <span>History of India</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>10</strong>
                <span>Geography of India</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>10</strong>
                <span>Polity &amp; Governance</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>10</strong>
                <span>Economy &amp; Sustainable Development</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>15</strong>
                <span>Science &amp; Technology</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>10</strong>
                <span>Jharkhand Specific</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>15</strong>
                <span>National &amp; International Current Events</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <strong>15</strong>
                <span>General Miscellaneous</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="small-note">
              The eight blocks above account for the 100-question Paper-I
              structure. The Commission's published syllabus describes the
              final 15 questions as general miscellaneous questions.
            </p>

            <h3>1. History of India — 15 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              History is divided into Ancient, Medieval and Modern India in
              the published syllabus, with five questions allocated to each
              period.
            </p>

            <div class="topic-columns">
              <div>
                <h4>Ancient India</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Indus Valley Civilization</li>
                  <li>Vedic period</li>
                  <li>Mahajanapadas</li>
                  <li>Buddhism and Jainism</li>
                  <li>Mauryan and Gupta periods</li>
                  <li>Ancient culture and heritage</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h4>Medieval India</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Early medieval developments</li>
                  <li>Delhi Sultanate</li>
                  <li>Mughal Empire</li>
                  <li>Administration and society</li>
                  <li>Bhakti and Sufi traditions</li>
                  <li>Medieval culture</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h4>Modern India</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>British expansion and administration</li>
                  <li>Revolt of 1857</li>
                  <li>Socio-religious reform movements</li>
                  <li>Indian nationalism</li>
                  <li>Gandhian movements</li>
                  <li>Constitutional developments and independence</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>2. Geography of India — 10 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              The official distribution divides Geography of India into
              General Geography, Physical Geography, Economic Geography and
              Social &amp; Demographic Geography.
            </p>

            <div class="topic-list">
              <div>
                <strong>General Geography</strong>
                <span>Location, regional patterns and basic geographic concepts.</span>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>Physical Geography</strong>
                <span>Physiography, climate, rivers, soils, vegetation and natural processes.</span>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>Economic Geography</strong>
                <span>Agriculture, minerals, industries, energy, transport and resources.</span>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>Social &amp; Demographic Geography</strong>
                <span>Population, distribution, migration, urbanization and demographic characteristics.</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>3. Indian Polity &amp; Governance — 10 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              The official distribution covers the Constitution of India,
              Public Administration and Good Governance, and decentralization
              through Panchayats and Municipalities.
            </p>

            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Constitutional development and features</li>
              <li>Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles</li>
              <li>Fundamental Duties</li>
              <li>President, Prime Minister and Council of Ministers</li>
              <li>Parliament and legislative procedure</li>
              <li>Supreme Court and High Courts</li>
              <li>Constitutional and statutory bodies</li>
              <li>Emergency provisions and amendments</li>
              <li>Public administration and accountability</li>
              <li>Panchayati Raj and municipalities</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>4. Economic &amp; Sustainable Development — 10 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              The published syllabus divides this area into the basic features
              of the Indian economy, sustainable development and economic
              issues, and economic reforms and globalization.
            </p>

            <div class="topic-columns two">
              <div>
                <h4>Indian Economy</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Growth and development</li>
                  <li>Poverty and unemployment</li>
                  <li>Inflation</li>
                  <li>Agriculture and industry</li>
                  <li>Banking and financial inclusion</li>
                  <li>Fiscal and monetary policy</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h4>Development</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Sustainable development</li>
                  <li>Economic reforms</li>
                  <li>Liberalization</li>
                  <li>Privatization</li>
                  <li>Globalization</li>
                  <li>Development challenges</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>5. Science &amp; Technology — 15 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              Science &amp; Technology carries 15 questions in the published
              Paper-I distribution. The three listed components are General
              Science, Agriculture &amp; Technology Development, and
              Information &amp; Communication Technology.
            </p>

            <div class="topic-list">
              <div>
                <strong>General Science</strong>
                <span>Basic Physics, Chemistry, Biology, health and everyday science.</span>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>Agriculture &amp; Technology Development</strong>
                <span>Modern agriculture, agricultural technology, biotechnology and related developments.</span>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>Information &amp; Communication Technology</strong>
                <span>Computers, internet, communication systems, digital technology and emerging technologies.</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>6. Jharkhand-Specific Questions — 10 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              Paper-I contains a dedicated Jharkhand-specific component covering
              general awareness of the state's history, society, culture and
              heritage. This is one of the clearest points where national
              preparation and state-specific preparation must meet.
            </p>

            <div class="jharkhand-box">
              <h4>Build your Jharkhand notebook around these blocks</h4>

              <div class="jharkhand-grid">
                <span>Jharkhand History</span>
                <span>Jharkhand Movement</span>
                <span>Tribes &amp; Society</span>
                <span>Culture &amp; Festivals</span>
                <span>Folk Literature</span>
                <span>Geography</span>
                <span>Economy &amp; Industries</span>
                <span>Land Laws</span>
                <span>Environment</span>
                <span>Government Schemes</span>
                <span>Current Affairs</span>
                <span>Important Personalities</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>7. National &amp; International Current Events — 15 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              Current affairs receives 15 questions in the official Paper-I
              distribution. Do not prepare current affairs as a disconnected
              news diary. Convert important events into exam categories.
            </p>

            <div class="current-grid">
              <div>
                <strong>National</strong>
                <ul>
                  <li>Government schemes and policies</li>
                  <li>Economy and reports</li>
                  <li>Science and technology</li>
                  <li>Environment</li>
                  <li>Defence</li>
                  <li>Awards and appointments</li>
                  <li>Sports</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>International</strong>
                <ul>
                  <li>International organizations</li>
                  <li>Summits and agreements</li>
                  <li>Global conflicts and diplomacy</li>
                  <li>Climate negotiations</li>
                  <li>International reports</li>
                  <li>India's international relations</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>Jharkhand Current Affairs</strong>
                <ul>
                  <li>State schemes</li>
                  <li>Budget and economy</li>
                  <li>Infrastructure</li>
                  <li>Mining and industry</li>
                  <li>Environment</li>
                  <li>Appointments and awards</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>8. General Miscellaneous Questions — 15 Questions</h3>

            <p>
              The published syllabus also includes a miscellaneous component
              of general nature that does not require subject specialization.
              Published reproductions of the syllabus commonly organize this
              area around themes such as human rights, environmental protection,
              biodiversity and climate change, urbanization, sports, disaster
              management, poverty and unemployment, awards, and the United
              Nations and other international agencies.
            </p>

            <div class="tag-cloud">
              <span>Human Rights</span>
              <span>Environment</span>
              <span>Biodiversity</span>
              <span>Climate Change</span>
              <span>Urbanization</span>
              <span>Sports</span>
              <span>Disaster Management</span>
              <span>Poverty</span>
              <span>Unemployment</span>
              <span>Awards</span>
              <span>UN &amp; International Agencies</span>
            </div>

            <h2 id="paper-two">JPSC Prelims Paper-II Syllabus</h2>

            <p>
              General Studies Paper-II is the strongly Jharkhand-focused part
              of the Preliminary Examination. It carries 200 marks and
              consists of 100 objective questions under the published
              examination structure.
            </p>

            <div class="source-note">
              <strong>Important:</strong>
              The detailed Paper-II topic headings below follow the published
              JPSC syllabus structure reproduced by established exam-reference
              sources. Before the 2027 examination, cross-check the latest
              Commission notification for any amendments.
            </div>

            <div class="paper-two-grid">
              <div>
                <span class="number">01</span>
                <h3>History of Jharkhand</h3>
                <p>
                  Traditional systems of governance and administration,
                  including Munda, Nagvanshi, Parha Panchayat, Manjhi-Pargana,
                  Munda-Manki and related customary institutions.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">02</span>
                <h3>Jharkhand Movement</h3>
                <p>
                  Tribal communities, freedom fighters, important leaders,
                  organizations, phases of the movement and the formation of
                  Jharkhand state.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">03</span>
                <h3>Distinct Identity of Jharkhand</h3>
                <p>
                  Social, cultural, political, economic and religious
                  characteristics that contribute to the identity of the state.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">04</span>
                <h3>Culture of Jharkhand</h3>
                <p>
                  Folk literature, traditional art, folk dances, folk music,
                  instruments, tourist places and tribal culture.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">05</span>
                <h3>Jharkhand Literature &amp; Writers</h3>
                <p>
                  Major literary traditions, languages, writers and important
                  contributions associated with Jharkhand.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">06</span>
                <h3>Educational Institutions</h3>
                <p>
                  Major educational and research institutions located in the
                  state and their significance.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">07</span>
                <h3>Sports of Jharkhand</h3>
                <p>
                  Important players, sports achievements, institutions,
                  events and sports-related facts connected with Jharkhand.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">08</span>
                <h3>Land Laws</h3>
                <p>
                  Chotanagpur Tenancy Act, Santhal Pargana Tenancy Act and other
                  relevant state land-law provisions.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">09</span>
                <h3>Economic Development &amp; Geography</h3>
                <p>
                  Economic development after 1947, physical and human
                  geography, resources and development patterns of Jharkhand.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">10</span>
                <h3>Industrial &amp; Rehabilitation Policies</h3>
                <p>
                  Industrial policy, displacement, rehabilitation and other
                  important state policies.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">11</span>
                <h3>Major Industries &amp; Locations</h3>
                <p>
                  Major industries, industrial locations, mineral resources and
                  industrial development in Jharkhand.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">12</span>
                <h3>Major Schemes &amp; Sub-Plans</h3>
                <p>
                  Major state schemes, development programmes and sub-plans
                  relevant to Jharkhand.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">13</span>
                <h3>Forest &amp; Wildlife</h3>
                <p>
                  Forest management, wildlife conservation, protected areas and
                  conservation initiatives.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">14</span>
                <h3>Environment</h3>
                <p>
                  Environmental facts, environmental change, mitigation,
                  adaptation and state-specific environmental issues.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">15</span>
                <h3>Disaster Management</h3>
                <p>
                  Disaster risks in Jharkhand, preparedness, mitigation,
                  response and management.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="number">16</span>
                <h3>Miscellaneous &amp; Current Affairs</h3>
                <p>
                  Important state facts and current events related to
                  Jharkhand.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="jharkhand">Why Jharkhand Should Be a Separate Preparation Track</h2>

            <p>
              JPSC is not simply a national General Studies examination with a
              few Jharkhand questions added at the end. The Preliminary scheme
              contains dedicated Jharkhand components, and Paper-II is
              strongly state-focused.
            </p>

            <div class="funnel">
              <div>
                <span>01</span>
                <strong>General Studies</strong>
                <small>Build national foundation</small>
              </div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div>
                <span>02</span>
                <strong>Jharkhand Core</strong>
                <small>Build state-specific depth</small>
              </div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div>
                <span>03</span>
                <strong>PYQs</strong>
                <small>See how JPSC asks</small>
              </div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div>
                <span>04</span>
                <strong>Mocks</strong>
                <small>Measure performance</small>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="pyq-strategy">How to Use JPSC PYQs With the Syllabus</h2>

            <p>
              Previous year questions should not be treated only as questions
              to solve after finishing the syllabus. They are one of the best
              tools for understanding how a broad syllabus becomes an actual
              JPSC question.
            </p>

            <div class="process-grid">
              <div>
                <strong>1</strong>
                <h3>Map the PYQ</h3>
                <p>Identify its paper, subject, topic and subtopic.</p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>2</strong>
                <h3>Classify it</h3>
                <p>Decide whether it is factual, conceptual, analytical or current-affairs based.</p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>3</strong>
                <h3>Study the concept</h3>
                <p>Read around the question rather than memorizing only its answer.</p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <strong>4</strong>
                <h3>Track repetition</h3>
                <p>Mark themes and concepts that appear repeatedly across papers.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="book-cta">
              <div>
                <div class="cta-label">JPSC PRELIMS PYQs</div>
                <h3>Turn the syllabus into a question map.</h3>
                <p>
                  Habitat IAS's <strong>JPSC Prelims Decoded: Topic-Wise
                  PYQs</strong> is designed to connect previous questions with
                  topics and explanations.
                </p>
              </div>

              <a href="/book/" class="button button-yellow">
                Explore JPSC PYQ Book →
              </a>
            </div>

            <h2 id="mock-strategy">How to Use Mock Tests for JPSC</h2>

            <p>
              A mock test is most useful when it produces information about
              your preparation. The objective is not merely to collect a high
              number of attempted tests.
            </p>

            <div class="analysis-grid">
              <div>
                <span class="analysis-icon">01</span>
                <h3>Score</h3>
                <p>Measure your current performance.</p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="analysis-icon">02</span>
                <h3>Accuracy</h3>
                <p>Find where you lose marks through incorrect answers.</p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="analysis-icon">03</span>
                <h3>Weak Topics</h3>
                <p>Identify subjects and topics requiring revision.</p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <span class="analysis-icon">04</span>
                <h3>Time</h3>
                <p>Check whether you can complete the paper comfortably.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="free-mock-cta">
              <div>
                <div class="cta-label">FREE JPSC PRACTICE</div>
                <h3>Don't just read the syllabus. Test it.</h3>
                <p>
